---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import quickstart from '../../../examples/quickstart.py?raw';
import Mermaid from '../../components/Mermaid.astro';
---

<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Quickstart Example' }}>
  <p>In this example, we demonstrate a trivial Pyrophone program to convey the principles of a node-based dataflow data processing architecture.</p>
  <Mermaid
    id="quickstart-diagram"
    label="Greeting emits a message, Uppercase transforms it, and Display prints it."
    source={`
flowchart LR
  Greeting@{ shape: "odd", label: "Greeting" } --> Uppercase["Uppercase\nTransform"] --> Display@{ shape: "curv-trap", label: "Display" }
`} />

  <p>This example will show us defining three custom node types:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a <code>Greeting</code> source node which emits a textual message (<code>hello, pyrophone</code>);</li>
    <li>an <code>Uppercase</code> transformation node which receives textual messages, converts them to uppercase, and then outputs them;</li>
    <li>a <code>Display</code> sink node which prints every message it receives on stdout.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Steps</h2>
  <p>Let's see how this can be accomplished in three easy steps:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>Install Pyrophone from <a href="https://pypi.org/project/Pyrophone/">PyPI</a>.</p>
      <Code lang="sh" code="python3 -m pip install Pyrophone" />
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>Create the file <code>quickstart.py</code>.</p>
      <Code lang="python" title="quickstart.py" code={quickstart} />
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>Run.</p>
      <Code lang="sh" code="python3 quickstart.py" />
      <Code lang="text" code={'HELLO, PYROPHONE\nHELLO, PYROPHONE\nHELLO, PYROPHONE'} />
      <p>The processing pipeline prints one message per second to stdout. Hit Ctrl-C to stop.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  :global(#quickstart-diagram > svg) {
    min-inline-size: 39rem;
  }
</style>
